    "segments": [
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 6,
        "sourceEnd": 180,
        "text": "Hi, everyone. Welcome to the Science Café. I'm Maya, and tonight we're talking about sleep and learning. Our guest is Dr. Sam Lee, a sleep researcher. Sam, thanks for coming.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-NatashaNeural",
        "textHash": "febc5e3b19bc108ccee2995c06c65f8ff39c0d5a812dcfc7a5b02bd62418cd3f",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0
      },
      {
        "role": "sam",
        "speaker": "Sam",
        "sourceStart": 186,
        "sourceEnd": 222,
        "text": "Thanks, Maya. It's great to be here.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-WilliamNeural",
        "textHash": "b0d64577749cbe9ea11a5bb7c6a62fd9bda8412b609213c94161544ee7ed37fd",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 229,
        "sourceEnd": 280,
        "text": "So, first question: how does sleep affect learning?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-NatashaNeural",
        "textHash": "a03ca7a7c4b53fa6e89615d8b3d9f3df7864451c3c6032b064deb487cd17e732",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "sam",
        "speaker": "Sam",
        "sourceStart": 286,
        "sourceEnd": 533,
        "text": "Well, that's a big question. Basically, sleep helps the brain consolidate memories. What I mean is that during sleep, the brain replays and strengthens what you learned during the day. So if you study before bed, you're more likely to remember it.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-WilliamNeural",
        "textHash": "9d4b52a2675a3ea4cf5dee682ed2996abd77fd7d101abceb424771b80cecf1f8",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 540,
        "sourceEnd": 597,
        "text": "Interesting. Does it matter which stage of sleep you get?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-NatashaNeural",
        "textHash": "253540fde43176a4206fa537b2384f10c94b45558fb148d238e865273db77716",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "sam",
        "speaker": "Sam",
        "sourceStart": 603,
        "sourceEnd": 807,
        "text": "Yes, it does. Deep sleep, which happens mostly in the first half of the night, is important for facts and figures. REM sleep, which comes later, helps with creative problem-solving and emotional memories.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-WilliamNeural",
        "textHash": "54aeaefa274a883dcbc37f15f68332d03095880c798ab0f09ff7f7c1bd3011e6",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 814,
        "sourceEnd": 883,
        "text": "So if someone pulls an all-nighter before an exam, that's a bad idea?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-NatashaNeural",
        "textHash": "59b70541835802249dbca85da54885f615601850d9dffe646368002afb3a93ed",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "sam",
        "speaker": "Sam",
        "sourceStart": 889,
        "sourceEnd": 1053,
        "text": "Definitely. You might think you're learning more by staying up, but actually you're reducing your ability to recall. It's better to sleep and review in the morning.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-WilliamNeural",
        "textHash": "1bfb419ee54e9679e57c8399c6e3111e0a916e1b8468089b5ea45364be8d777b",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 1060,
        "sourceEnd": 1094,
        "text": "What about naps? Are they helpful?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-NatashaNeural",
        "textHash": "959fd4fb8e29b3608f1ea23094dfaaad2875883b1f20d390acd197a50bcef585",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "sam",
        "speaker": "Sam",
        "sourceStart": 1100,
        "sourceEnd": 1242,
        "text": "Short naps can be, yes. A 20-minute nap can boost alertness. But if you nap for more than 30 minutes, you might feel groggy. So keep it short.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-WilliamNeural",
        "textHash": "63c7380534ca4e6c0ca26fe398a6babcc6b9d32c8f22b349a3330086cb29bc7f",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 1249,
        "sourceEnd": 1288,
        "text": "And does the time you go to bed matter?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-NatashaNeural",
        "textHash": "fcf1a07dc54f071e87a5414c5c2f116758352c2714890472a18719322b0bd7dd",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "sam",
        "speaker": "Sam",
        "sourceStart": 1294,
        "sourceEnd": 1472,
        "text": "Consistency is key. Going to bed and waking up at the same time every day, even on weekends, helps your body clock. If you change your schedule a lot, your sleep quality suffers.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-WilliamNeural",
        "textHash": "9fbbbc2157553a7194406ff8fb05b372bfbdf6ce2215eb3748ddcbc5a6166a7f",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 1479,
        "sourceEnd": 1580,
        "text": "Some people say they can learn during sleep, like listening to recordings while asleep. Is that true?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-NatashaNeural",
        "textHash": "49c4b27858d9ec8944a868ab38a1103e31741bdeb0ddf3c18dbf800224d7c47c",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "sam",
        "speaker": "Sam",
        "sourceStart": 1586,
        "sourceEnd": 1799,
        "text": "That's a common myth. You can't learn new information during sleep. But sleep does help you retain what you learned before. So listening to recordings while asleep won't help you learn a new language, for example.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-WilliamNeural",
        "textHash": "f6f8ab084adbefc68b06feca7017306fe1cdc6c63b6ce300f9e6288ee2d4c3da",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 1806,
        "sourceEnd": 1855,
        "text": "OK, so what's one practical tip for our audience?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-NatashaNeural",
        "textHash": "2df531a6ec780badcae1a9b233d9807430e36d9cab4543c8904c68d97819b516",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "sam",
        "speaker": "Sam",
        "sourceStart": 1861,
        "sourceEnd": 2012,
        "text": "Try to review material before bed, and then get a full night's sleep. And avoid screens for an hour before bed, because the blue light can delay sleep.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-WilliamNeural",
        "textHash": "815d2adca2d527e4916c96d600c496cf00caa5de8add1ad1cdfcfc326b2f0ce9",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 2019,
        "sourceEnd": 2045,
        "text": "Great advice. Thanks, Sam.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-NatashaNeural",
        "textHash": "61d097c88fce1535ea062f766a24807207bff1798a409397cf8fdb9f0d8fad3a",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "sam",
        "speaker": "Sam",
        "sourceStart": 2051,
        "sourceEnd": 2063,
        "text": "My pleasure.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-WilliamNeural",
        "textHash": "c4a93eaaa5a80f6820bfefd0aa85ba20402b532c89f2635760c00d7e7871215e",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      }
    ]
